  "verbalization": "Thiotepa, through its inclusion in the Alkylating Activity category, is linked to multiple antineoplastic agents such as Chlorambucil and Melphalan, both of which are known to treat Breast Cancer. This suggests a potential shared mechanism of action involving alkylating agents, which are commonly used in cancer therapy to disrupt DNA and inhibit tumor growth. The evidence from these paths highlights a recurring role of alkylating agents in targeting breast cancer, with Thiotepa being a relevant member of this class. The lowest common ancestors of Thiotepa and these agents, both classified as Organic Molecular Entities and Antineoplastic Alkylating Agents, further support a common biological pathway. While the exact mechanism of Thiotepa's antitumor effect remains to be fully elucidated, the integration of these pathways suggests that Thiotepa may exert its therapeutic effect through a mechanism similar to other alkylating agents, potentially by inducing DNA damage or interfering with cellular replication in breast cancer cells.",
